The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!(ac->ac_flags & EXT4_MB_DELALLOC_RESERVED))
    percpu_counter_sub(&sbi->s_dirtyclusters_counter, reserv_clstrs);
```
and
```
if (!ar->len) {
    if ((ar->flags & EXT4_MB_DELALLOC_RESERVED) == 0)
        percpu_counter_sub(&sbi->s_dirtyclusters_counter, reserv_clstrs);
}
```
Reason:
When `ext4_mb_mark_diskspace_used()` fails (e.g., due to a shutdown-induced error), it still decrements `s_dirtyclusters_counter` for non‑delalloc allocations. The caller `ext4_mb_new_blocks()` then enters the `errout` path, sets `ar->len = 0`, and later at the `out` label (where `!ar->len` is true) decrements the same counter again. This double decrement causes the counter to go negative, triggering a warning in `ext4_put_super()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
/* remove the decrement from ext4_mb_mark_diskspace_used */
/* (entire block of code removed) */

/* replace the out‑label decrement with an unconditional one */
if (reserv_clstrs)
    percpu_counter_sub(&sbi->s_dirtyclusters_counter, reserv_clstrs);
```
Reason:
By removing the decrement from `ext4_mb_mark_diskspace_used()` and moving it to a single, unconditional location in the caller (`ext4_mb_new_blocks()`), the dirty clusters counter is decremented exactly once regardless of whether the allocation succeeds or fails. This eliminates the double‑decrement scenario because the counter reduction is now solely the responsibility of the caller, which always executes the new code block when `reserv_clstrs` is non‑zero.

